:title("Java (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a Java subset to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

The subset: classes with fields (instance and static, with initializers), methods
(instance and static), constructors, single inheritance (class B extends A: inherited
fields and methods, overriding with dynamic dispatch, the implicit no argument super
constructor, and super.m() calls; a class must appear before its subclasses),
constructor OVERLOADING (resolved by argument count) and the explicit constructor
invocations super(args) and this(args), static and instance initializer blocks,
records (auto generated constructor, accessors and value equality, plus the compact
constructor and extra constructors), enums (constants with arguments, constant
specific bodies, values / valueOf / name / ordinal), interfaces (constants, default,
private and static methods, Iface.super.m() and multiple inheritance of defaults),
annotation types, nested / inner / local / anonymous classes (an inner instance keeps
its enclosing one, reached with Outer.this and created with o.new Inner(...)),
lambdas and method references (including Type::new) as real closures, SAM conversion
when the declared type is a functional interface,
object creation with new, arrays (new int[n], the multi dimensional new int[a][b]
and the partial new int[a][], bare initializers like {1, 2}, C style declarators,
a.length, indexing), varargs (a trailing array parameter, fed either by the extra
arguments or by an array), the enhanced for loop
(for (var x : arr)), int / boolean / char / String / var / class types (types are
parsed; the semantics are dynamic, but char is a value of its own that prints as its
glyph and computes on its code), String concatenation with +, the String methods
length(), charAt(), equals(), substring(), indexOf() and isEmpty() (charAt yields a
char, so it prints as its glyph and computes on its code),
System.out.println/print, System.exit, Math.abs/max/min,
if/else, while, do-while, both for forms, the classic colon switch on int or String
(fallthrough, stacked labels, default) and the arrow switch statement (case 1, 2 ->
expr/block, no fallthrough), switch as an EXPRESSION with yield in both forms, type
and record deconstruction patterns in case labels with when guards, the instanceof
pattern forms, break/continue (labeled too: labeled loops, labeled
blocks; a label inside a try body cannot jump out), return, assignment (also
+= -= *= /= %= and the bitwise/shift compounds &= |= ^= <<= >>= >>>=), postfix ++/--,
the ternary operator, || && with short circuit, & | ^ (non-short-circuit boolean
operators on two booleans, int32 bit operations otherwise), << >> >>> (Java int
semantics), == != < > <= >= (== compares objects by identity, use equals() for
strings), + - * / % (int arithmetic is 32 bit, / truncates) and unary - ! ~.

Literals: decimal / hex / binary / octal / underscored / suffixed integers, the
fractional, exponent and hexadecimal floating point forms, char literals with Java's
escape set (the single letter ones, the quotes, the four digit unicode form and the
classic octal escapes) and TEXT BLOCKS with the JLS incidental white space rules.

try / catch (multi catch A | B) / finally / throw are genuinely modelled: a throw
raises the value and the nearest try recovers it, a return/break/continue leaving a
try body is re issued in the enclosing frame, and try-with-resources closes its
resources in reverse order, whether the body completes or throws. The standard
throwable classes the program names (Throwable, Exception, RuntimeException, Error
and the common subclasses) are provided by the compile, with a message constructor
and getMessage(); a class may extend them.

Widened surface (parsed so a whole Java file is accepted): generics (<...>) and the
extra primitive keywords are parsed and dropped; casts ((Type) expr) are identity;
annotations (with or without arguments), TYPE ANNOTATIONS in every position JLS
9.7.4 allows, throws clauses, sealed / non-sealed and the permits clause, qualified
and generic supertypes, several declarators per field, array class literals and the
stray semicolon after a type declaration are parsed and ignored. assert and
synchronized are recognized and reported as not implemented; -warn-unsupported
downgrades that to a warning so call graphs / CFGs / traces still work on partly
understood files.

Unicode escapes are translated in PHASE ONE, before the scanner runs (JLS 3.3),
so a unicode escape anywhere in the file - in an identifier, in a keyword, in a
comment - is the character it denotes. An IMPLICITLY DECLARED class (JEP 512) is
accepted: a file that declares members but no class becomes a synthetic class
Main, whose `main` may be an instance method (the launcher constructs it).

Not genuinely modelled: overload resolution by argument TYPE (only the count
discriminates), generic type checking (type arguments are erased at parse time),
implicit FIELD access (a field is reached via this.x or ClassName.x - like the
explicit style many code bases enforce; only calls resolve unqualified), checked
exception analysis, modules, threads and reflection.

Fields live directly on the instance object (js_get/js_set), methods become own IR
functions jsf_N(env, args) with the instance prepended to the arguments, stored on the
class descriptor object; calls dispatch through the js_mcall external. A constructor
always exists (a default one is generated) and first applies the field initializers;
an overloaded class also gets a dispatcher that picks the arity matching slot.
The whole program becomes jsmain(env, args): it builds the class descriptors, then calls
Main.main. Self checking programs end with System.exit(failures).
The same language with the same semantics is interpreted directly by java-interpreter.abnf.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident-dollar.abnf") ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = UniPre [ Package ] { Import } { ModuleDecl | UnsupType | ClassDecl | RecordDecl | StraySemi } [ ImplicitClass ] ;
// A stray ';' after a top-level type declaration (`class F { };`) is legal and
// means nothing - the member-level form is EmptyMember below.
StraySemi   = ";" ;

// ----- JEP 512: an IMPLICITLY DECLARED class. A compilation unit that declares
// no class at all but does declare members is one class body without the
// `class X {` around it: `void main() { ... }` alone in a file is a program.
// The members are built into a synthetic class named Main, which is exactly the
// class this grammar's entry point looks for, and its `main` may be an INSTANCE
// method - the launcher instantiates the class when the static lookup misses.
// It is the LAST element of Program, so an ordinary file consumes everything
// through the declaration repetition and reaches it at EOF, where Member fails
// at once and the optional is skipped.
ImplicitClass <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ImplicitHead Member { Member } ;
ImplicitHead <~~ push("Main"); jEnterClass("Main") ~~>
            = NotTypeKw ;

// The implicit class may not START with a type declaration: a file whose first
// top-level item is `class` / `record` / `interface` / `enum` / `@interface` is
// an ordinary compilation unit, and if that declaration does not parse the file
// is broken - it is not a member of an implicit class. Without this, a record
// javac refuses (`record R(final int x) {}`) matched Method (`record` as the
// return type, `R(...)` as the signature, `{}` as the body) and five reject
// files were accepted for eight parse files gained; with it, the eight stay and
// the five come back. The modifier run is walked exactly as NoDupMods walks it.
NotTypeKw   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var done = false
        var bad = false
        var rounds = 0
        while (!done && rounds < 64) {
            rounds++
            var moving = true
            while (moving) {
                var ch = c.peek(i)
                if (ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 10 || ch === 13 || ch === 12) { i++ }
                else if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i++ } }
                else if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                    i = i + 2
                    var g = 0
                    while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && g < 40000) {
                        if (c.peek(i) === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { i = i + 2; g = 40000 } else { i++; g++ }
                    }
                } else { moving = false }
            }
            if (c.peek(i) === 64) {            // an annotation, or `@interface`
                i++
                var ws = 0
                while (c.peek(i + ws) === 32 || c.peek(i + ws) === 9 || c.peek(i + ws) === 10 || c.peek(i + ws) === 13) { ws++ }
                var iw = "interface"
                var isAt = true
                for (var q = 0; q < iw.length; q++) { if (c.peek(i + ws + q) !== iw.charCodeAt(q)) { isAt = false } }
                if (isAt) { bad = true; done = true }
                else {
                    var an = 0
                    while (an < 4000) {
                        var ac = c.peek(i)
                        if ((ac >= 48 && ac <= 57) || (ac >= 65 && ac <= 90) || ac === 95 || ac === 36 || ac === 46 || (ac >= 97 && ac <= 122)) { i++; an++ } else { an = 4000 }
                    }
                    var ms = 0
                    while (c.peek(i + ms) === 32 || c.peek(i + ms) === 9 || c.peek(i + ms) === 10 || c.peek(i + ms) === 13) { ms++ }
                    if (c.peek(i + ms) === 40) {
                        i = i + ms + 1
                        var d = 1
                        var g3 = 0
                        while (d > 0 && g3 < 40000 && c.peek(i) >= 0) {
                            var pc = c.peek(i)
                            if (pc === 40) { d++ } else if (pc === 41) { d-- }
                            i++
                            g3++
                        }
                    }
                }
            } else {
                var n = 0
                var more = true
                while (more) {
                    var wc = c.peek(i + n)
                    if ((wc >= 97 && wc <= 122) || wc === 45) { n++ } else { more = false }
                    if (n > 12) { more = false }
                }
                var mods = ["public", "private", "protected", "static", "final",
                            "abstract", "synchronized", "native", "transient",
                            "volatile", "strictfp", "sealed", "non-sealed", "default"]
                var types = ["class", "record", "interface", "enum"]
                var hit = false
                for (var k = 0; k < mods.length; k++) {
                    var w = mods[k]
                    if (w.length === n) {
                        var ok = true
                        for (var j = 0; j < n; j++) { if (c.peek(i + j) !== w.charCodeAt(j)) { ok = false } }
                        if (ok) { hit = true }
                    }
                }
                for (var k2 = 0; k2 < types.length; k2++) {
                    var w2 = types[k2]
                    if (w2.length === n) {
                        var ok2 = true
                        for (var j2 = 0; j2 < n; j2++) { if (c.peek(i + j2) !== w2.charCodeAt(j2)) { ok2 = false } }
                        if (ok2) { bad = true }
                    }
                }
                if (hit && !bad) { i = i + n } else { done = true }
            }
        }
        return bad ? abnf.newToken("\x01type-declaration", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// ----- Phase ONE of the Java compilation pipeline (JLS 3.3): a unicode escape
// is translated into the character it denotes BEFORE the scanner runs, so
// `public class X {}` really is `public class X {}` and a `\` really
// is a backslash the scanner then reads as an escape. It is a source PRE-PASS,
// not a grammar rule, and it is expressed as one: the script reads the whole
// source with c.getSrc(), rewrites it and hands it back with c.setSrc(), then
// restarts the parse position - the same shape as C's -pipe preprocessor stage,
// but inside one grammar so the corpus row measures it.
// A backslash is ELIGIBLE only when the run of backslashes ending at it has ODD
// length (JLS: an even number of preceding backslashes), so `"\\u0041"` stays
// the six characters A; the eligible one may then be followed by any
// number of `u` and must be followed by exactly four hex digits.
// The whole pass leaves after one indexOf on a file that contains no `\u`,
// which is every file but a handful.
UniPre      = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var src = c.getSrc()
        if (src.indexOf("\\u") < 0) { return undefined }
        var n = src.length
        var out = ""
        var last = 0      // start of the not-yet-copied run
        var i = 0
        var changed = false
        while (i < n) {
            if (src.charAt(i) !== "\\") { i++; continue }
            var j = i
            while (j < n && src.charAt(j) === "\\") { j++ }
            var odd = ((j - i) % 2) === 1
            if (odd && j < n && src.charAt(j) === "u") {
                var k = j
                while (k < n && src.charAt(k) === "u") { k++ }
                var hex = src.substring(k, k + 4)
                var ok = hex.length === 4
                for (var h = 0; h < hex.length; h++) {
                    var hc = hex.charCodeAt(h)
                    if (!((hc >= 48 && hc <= 57) || (hc >= 97 && hc <= 102) || (hc >= 65 && hc <= 70))) { ok = false }
                }
                if (ok) {
                    // Everything before the eligible backslash is copied as it
                    // stands - that includes the (j - i - 1) literal ones.
                    out += src.substring(last, j - 1)
                    out += String.fromCharCode(parseInt(hex, 16))
                    last = k + 4
                    i = k + 4
                    changed = true
                    continue
                }
            }
            i = j
        }
        if (!changed) { return undefined }
        out += src.substring(last, n)
        c.setSrc(out)
        c.setSdx(0)
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// --- module-info.java: a JPMS module declaration (JLS 7.7). Modules are not
// modelled, and there is nothing in one to compile, so the whole declaration is
// pure syntax: no tag anywhere, so it pushes no statement and buildMain never
// sees it. Kept identical to the interpreter half's copy.
//
// `module`, `open`, `requires`, `transitive`, `exports`, `opens`, `to`, `uses`,
// `provides` and `with` are RESTRICTED keywords - ordinary identifiers
// everywhere else in the language - so each is matched as a bare literal HERE
// and is never added to ModKw or to any Kw* rule.
ModuleDecl  = LeadMods [ "open" KwEnd ] "module" KwEnd ModName "{" { ModDirective } "}" ;
// A module / package / service name is dotted, and nothing consumes it, so this
// is QualName without QualName's push.
ModName     = Id { "." Id } ;
ModDirective = ModRequires | ModExports | ModOpens | ModUses | ModProvides | StraySemi ;
ModRequires = "requires" KwEnd { ReqMod } ModName ";" ;
ReqMod      = ( "transitive" | "static" ) KwEnd ;
ModExports  = "exports" KwEnd ModName [ ModTo ] ";" ;
ModOpens    = "opens" KwEnd ModName [ ModTo ] ";" ;
ModTo       = "to" KwEnd ModName { "," ModName } ;
ModUses     = "uses" KwEnd ModName ";" ;
ModProvides = "provides" KwEnd ModName "with" KwEnd ModName { "," ModName } ";" ;

// An optional package header (parsed and ignored) then repeated imports. Each
// import path is resolved against the runtime builtins (core.stdlibImports); one
// that does not resolve aborts the compile, unless -warn-imports downgrades it to
// a warning (see resolveImport in compile-core.js). 'import static a.b.C.d'
// resolves the dotted path too. QualName also accepts a trailing '.*' wildcard.
// Neither Package nor Import pushes a statement, so buildMain never sees them.
// package-info.java carries its annotations on the package declaration.
Package     <~~ pop() ~~> = LeadMods KwPackage QualName ";" ;
// `import module java.base;` (JEP 476) imports every package a module exports.
// It is the SECOND alternative on purpose: `module` is a restricted keyword, so
// `import module.foo.Bar;` is still a legal ordinary import of a package called
// `module`, and trying the plain form first keeps that reading.
Import      <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = KwImport [ "static" KwEnd ] QualName ";"
            | KwImport "module" KwEnd QualName ";" ;
QualName    <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> = Id { "." Id } [ "." "*" ] ;

ClassDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] KwClass Id <~~ push(up.in); jEnterClass(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ]
              [ KwExtends ExtRef ]
              [ ImplementsSkip ] [ PermitsSkip ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// A supertype may be qualified and generic (`extends Outer.Super`, `extends
// A<B>.C`). Nested types are hoisted under their SIMPLE name, so the qualifier
// only has to be accepted - the last segment names the descriptor.
ExtRef      <~~ push({ext: jLastSeg(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = { Annotation } Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] { "." { Annotation } Id <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] } ;

// record Pair(int a, int b) { } -- generates fields, a constructor and accessors.
RecordDecl  <~~ push(makeRecordStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] KwRecord Id <~~ push(up.in); jEnterClass(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] RParams
              [ ImplementsSkip ] [ PermitsSkip ] "{" { RMember } "}" ;
// A record body is restricted (JLS 8.10.4): no INSTANCE initializer
// (instance.initializer.not.allowed.in.records) and no instance FIELD
// (record.cannot.declare.instance.fields). Both are simply absent here -
// InitBlock is gone, and a field must carry `static`, which HasStatic demands.
// An interface has no instance initializer (initializer.not.allowed, JLS 9.1.1):
// InitBlock is simply absent from its member set.
IMember     = EmptyMember | NestedType | StaticInit | Ctor | AbstractMethod
            | CompactCtor | Method | Field ;
RMember     = EmptyMember | NestedType | StaticInit | Ctor | AbstractMethod
            | CompactCtor | Method | RField ;
RField      <~~ pushFields(takeAll()) ~~>
            = HasStatic Mods FType FieldVar { "," FieldVar } ";" ;
RParams     <~~ push({rparams: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ RParam { "," RParam } ] ")" ;
// A record component may carry annotations and may be VARARGS (JLS 8.10.1);
// the `...` is parsed and dropped - the component binds the single argument.
RParam      = { Annotation } Type { Annotation } [ "..." ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// A stray ';' between members is legal Java (an empty declaration) and pushes nothing.
Member      = EmptyMember | NestedType | StaticInit | InitBlock | Ctor | AbstractMethod
            | CompactCtor | Method | Field ;
EmptyMember = ";" ;

// A static initializer - `static { ... }` - runs when the class is built, in textual
// order among the static field initializers. An instance initializer - a bare
// `{ ... }` in the class body - runs on every construction, in textual order among
// the INSTANCE field initializers and before the constructor body; both orders fall
// out of the member list being walked in order.
StaticInit  <~~ push({kind: "staticinit", body: pop()}) ~~>
            = "static" KwEnd Block ;
InitBlock   <~~ push({kind: "initblock", body: pop()}) ~~>
            = Block ;

// A bodiless method declaration: `abstract int wheels();` in an abstract class, or a
// method of an interface. It contributes no code - the overriding subclass supplies
// it - so the tag drains what the header pushed and the member list never sees it.
AbstractMethod <~~ push(makeAbstract(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods [ SkipAngle ] Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ CDims ] [ Throws ] ";" ;

// A constructor. Its body is CtorBody rather than Block so the optional explicit
// constructor invocation - super(args) / this(args), which Java only admits as the
// first statement - stays visible to makeCtor: a `super(args)` replaces the implicit
// no-argument super call, and a `this(args)` also suppresses the field initializers
// (they run once, in the constructor that finally reaches super).
Ctor        <~~ push(makeCtor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] [ SkipAngle ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ Throws ] CtorBody ;
// A record's COMPACT constructor - `Range { ... }`, no parameter list: the record
// components are the parameters, the body may reassign them, and the fields are
// assigned from them afterwards (see buildClassStmt).
CompactCtor <~~ push(makeCompactCtor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LeadMods ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> CtorBody ;
CtorBody    <~~ push({cbody: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" [ CtorDelegate ] { Statement } "}" ;
CtorDelegate <~~ push(makeCtorDelegate(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( KwSuper <~~ push("super") ~~> | KwThis <~~ push("this") ~~> )
              "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" ";" ;

Method      <~~ push(makeMethod(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Mods [ SkipAngle ] Type Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList [ CDims ] [ Throws ] Block ;

// One `int a, b = 2, c;` declares several fields, which share their modifiers
// and type but not their initializer - so each declarator becomes its own member.
Field       <~~ pushFields(takeAll()) ~~>
            = Mods FType FieldVar { "," FieldVar } ";" ;
FieldVar    <~~ push(makeFieldVar(takeAll())) ~~>
            = NotResvKw NotUscore Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ CDims ] [ "=" Expression ] ;
// The C-style declarator suffix: `int a[]` and `int[] a[]` declare arrays too.
CDims       = { Annotation } "[" "]" { { Annotation } "[" "]" } ;

// Modifiers before a member: annotations (with or without arguments) are parsed and
// ignored; ModWord keeps the modifier text so `static` is still detectable.
Mods        <~~ push({mods: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = NoDupMods { Annotation | ModWord } ;
ModWord     <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = ModKw ;
FType       <~~ push({ty: up.in}) ~~>
            = NotVarKw Type ;

// A parameter may carry annotations and `final` (both ignored). A VARARGS parameter
// (`int... xs`) is real: it collects the remaining arguments into an array, and an
// array handed in its place is passed straight through, like in Java.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ParamOk "(" [ Param { "," Param } ] ")" ;
// RecvParam is the JLS 8.4.1 RECEIVER parameter (`void m(Foo Foo.this)`) - a
// declaration of the type of `this`, not a real parameter, so it pushes nothing.
// It has to come FIRST: the ordinary form matches `Foo Foo` and dies on `.this`.
Param       = RecvParam
            | { Annotation | "final" KwEnd } Type { Annotation } [ "..." <~~ push({vararg: true}) ~~> ]
              Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { { Annotation } "[" "]" } ;
RecvParam   = { Annotation } Type { Id "." } "this" KwEnd ;

// Types are parsed and (apart from field defaults) ignored. Generic arguments
// (<...>), dotted names, extra array dimensions and the extra primitive keywords are
// accepted and dropped. Semantics stay dynamic.
// A TYPE ANNOTATION may sit in front of the type, in front of any segment of a
// qualified one, and in front of each array bracket pair (`@A String @A []`,
// `Outer.@A Inner`) - JLS 9.7.4. They are dropped like every other annotation.
Type        = { Annotation } ( PrimName KwEnd | "String" KwEnd | "void" KwEnd | "var" KwEnd NoBracket
              | NotResvKw NotVarArr Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." { Annotation } Id [ SkipAngle ] } ) { { Annotation } "[" "]" } ;
PrimName    = "int" | "boolean" | "char" | "long" | "double" | "float" | "short" | "byte" ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
Statement   <~~ push(stmtPos(pop())) ~~>
            = Block | If | While | Do | ForEach | For | Switch | Return | Yield | Break | Continue
            | Try | Throw | Assert | Synchronized | CtorCall | LocalType | Labeled | Decl
            | Empty | ExprStmt ;

// A LOCAL type declaration - `class Local { ... }` inside a method body. It lowers
// exactly like a top-level one, into the enclosing block's scope, so it sees the
// method's locals. A local class may not be `sealed` / `non-sealed` (JLS 8.1.1.2).
LocalType   = NoSealed TypeDecl ;

// JLS 14.5: the body of an if / loop / labeled statement is a *Statement*, and a
// local variable declaration and a local class declaration are NOT Statements -
// they are BlockStatements, which only a block admits. `if (true) int x = 0;` and
// `if (true) class X {}` are what javac rejects with variable.not.allowed /
// class.not.allowed, and SubStmt is exactly Statement minus those two. It carries
// Statement's own tag so a sub-statement still gets its js_srcpos marker.
SubStmt     <~~ push(stmtPos(pop())) ~~>
            = Block | If | While | Do | ForEach | For | Switch | Return | Yield | Break | Continue
            | Try | Throw | Assert | Synchronized | CtorCall | Labeled | Empty | ExprStmt ;

Block       <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;

If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf "(" Expression ")" SubStmt [ KwElse SubStmt ] ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhile(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwWhile "(" Expression ")" SubStmt ;

Do          <~~ push(makeDo(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = KwDo SubStmt KwWhile "(" Expression ")" ";" ;

// The classic colon switch (on int or String): execution starts at the first matching
// label and runs until a break or the end of the switch. Labels may stack.
Switch      <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSwitch "(" Expression ")" "{" { SwitchCase } "}" ;
// The arrow forms (case 1, 2 -> body) are sugar for stacked classic labels
// whose shared body ends in an implicit break: no fallthrough, several labels
// per arm. Tried first; they fail fast at ':' for the classic forms.
SwitchCase  = ArrowCase | ArrowDefault | Case | Default ;
ArrowCase   <~~ pushArrowCase(takeAll()) ~~>
            = KwCase NotDfltKw ALabel { "," ALabel } "->" ArrowBody ;
ALabel      <~~ push({alab: makeArrowLabel(pop(), stripWs(up.in))}) ~~>
            = ALabCore ;
// A label that is a bare name directly before the arrow - `case S -> ...` - has to be
// read as the name, because the expression grammar would otherwise take `S -> ...`
// for a lambda (LambdaHead accepts a bare identifier). IdArrowAhead is the guard that
// tells the two apart; every other label shape goes through the ordinary expression.
ALabCore    = ALabName | PatLabel | CondExpr ;

// A PATTERN label: `case Dot d ->`, `case Integer i when i > 10 ->`, and the record
// deconstruction forms `case Dot(int x) ->` / `case Line(Dot(var x1), Dot d2) ->`.
// A type pattern REQUIRES its binding name (Java says so), which is exactly what
// keeps `case CONSTANT ->` from being read as one.
PatLabel    <~~ push({plab: makePatLabel(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = Pattern [ WhenClause ] ;
